# Solis–Wets

Solis–Wets is a random local search that adapts its step size and remembers
successful directions. It tests a Gaussian perturbation and, if that fails,
its reversal. It accepts a new point only when the objective improves, using
objective values without derivatives or a matrix model.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/solis_wets/struct.SolisWets.html)

| Property | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Objective | A scalar function |
| Evaluations | `CostFunction` |
| Search | Random local search from one starting point |
| Constraints | Unconstrained; declarations are ignored |
| Parameters | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64` |

## When to use it

Consider Solis–Wets when derivatives are unavailable and a simple local
search with `O(n)` storage suits the evaluation budget. The method adapts a
single mutation scale and a direction bias, keeping its vector work linear
in the number of parameters. It belongs to the random-search framework of
[Solis and Wets (1981)](#ref-solis-wets-1981).

For a smooth problem with a small number of expensive evaluations, compare
the quadratic interpolation model in [NEWUOA](../newuoa/).
[Nelder–Mead](../nelder-mead/) offers deterministic simplex moves, while
[MADS](../mads/) uses polling on a mesh. Solis–Wets is useful when its low
storage cost and randomized directions matter more than building a local
curvature model.

Treat it as a local method. Random directions do not ensure that a finite
run finds a global minimum, and strict improvement prevents the method from
crossing an objective barrier through worse points. Use multiple starts
when you need to compare local solutions.

## How it works

The solver starts with zero bias and a mutation standard deviation `rho` of
`1`. Each iteration samples independent normal components with that standard
deviation and adds the bias to form a step. It evaluates the forward point
first. If that point does not strictly improve the current cost, it evaluates
the reversed step. A forward success retains `0.2` of the old bias and adds
`0.4` times the successful step. A reversal success subtracts `0.4` times
the forward step from the bias. When both trials fail, the bias is halved.

Five consecutive successful iterations double `rho`; three consecutive
failed iterations halve it. Each adaptation resets its corresponding
streak counter. An iteration therefore costs one objective evaluation on
forward success and two otherwise. The current objective never increases.

Basin interprets `rho` as the per-coordinate standard deviation and resets
streaks after adaptation, following the conventions used in memetic local
search implementations. These details differ from a literal reading of
the covariance notation and counter placement in the original paper.

## Example

This quadratic has its minimum at `[1.0, -2.0]`. A fixed seed makes the run
reproducible. The solver stops when its mutation standard deviation reaches
`1e-8`, and the executor also imposes an iteration budget.

<!-- compile: solver-solis-wets -->
```rust
use basin::{CostFunction, Executor, SolisWets, Termination};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Quadratic;

impl CostFunction for Quadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x[0] - 1.0).powi(2) + 4.0 * (x[1] + 2.0).powi(2))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let solver = SolisWets::new(42)
        .with_initial_step_size(1.0)
        .with_absolute_step_size_tolerance(1e-8);

    let result = Executor::from_start(Quadratic, solver, vec![-3.0, 5.0])
        .max_iter(10_000)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(matches!(
        result.report.termination,
        Termination::Converged(_)
    ));
    assert!(result.cost() < 1e-12);
    assert!((result.param()[0] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-6);
    assert!((result.param()[1] + 2.0).abs() < 1e-6);
}
```

## Practical considerations

### Set the initial scale in parameter units

`with_initial_step_size` controls the initial standard deviation in every
coordinate. Choose it for the distance you expect to travel and rescale
parameters with different units. A single scalar scale cannot independently
adapt to different sensitivities along each coordinate. The bias gain,
memory, decay, adaptation thresholds, and expansion and contraction factors
all have `with_*` setters, but parameter scaling is a useful first adjustment.

### Choose a stopping rule and budget

Solis–Wets has no default convergence test. Configure
`with_absolute_step_size_tolerance` to stop on a small mutation standard
deviation. `None` disables this check, and zero requests exact collapse.
A small `rho` reports a small search scale; it does not certify stationarity
or a particular parameter error. Optional step- and cost-change checks
observe accepted moves, so rejected trials do not turn an unchanged point
into convergence.

Keep execution limits on the executor and inspect the termination report.
An iteration uses up to two evaluations, so a cost budget checked at
iteration boundaries can be exceeded by one. Noisy evaluations can also
make strict improvement misleading; a lucky low observation can prevent
subsequent proposals from being accepted.

### Handle constraints explicitly

Solis–Wets does not project or clip candidates, and constraint declarations
do not restrict its search. Use an explicit adapter or choose a constrained
solver such as [BOBYQA](../bobyqa/) for box bounds. An objective can return
`Ok(f64::INFINITY)` for an infeasible candidate: from a finite incumbent,
that candidate fails the improvement test. This rejection policy is useful
only when the objective and feasible region make it suitable; it does not
turn Solis–Wets into a general constrained solver.

## Implementation

`PointState` publishes the current point and its cost. The solver retains
the bias, adapted step size, success and failure counters, and a seeded
`ChaCha8Rng`. Retain it through `Executor::run_with_solver` to inspect
`step_size()`, `bias()`, `success_count()`, and `failure_count()`.

The same seed reproduces the same trajectory for the same deterministic
objective. Fresh initialization restores that seed, the configured initial
scale, zero bias, and empty streaks, then evaluates the starting point.
An exact solver-and-state checkpoint preserves the RNG and adaptation and
skips initialization; a state-only snapshot starts a fresh search.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. The method
requires normal sampling and basic vector operations, without a matrix
factorization or BLAS/LAPACK provider. See the API's [Backends
section](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/solis_wets/struct.SolisWets.html#backends)
for custom-vector requirements.

